21:40:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your concern is fair: there is no single coordinated “IPv4 replacement day,” and the transition has been slow and uneven. But the Internet already needs far more endpoint addresses than IPv4 can provide. IPv4 has about 4.3 billion numerical addresses, while there are far more phones, computers, IoT devices, and virtual machines. We handle that today largely because most devices do **not** get their own globally routable IPv4 address. The actual plan is a combination of technologies and incentives rather than one central event: - **IPv6 provides more addresses.** It is the real capacity expansion: its address space is so large that practical exhaustion is not a concern. - **Dual-stack networks run IPv4 and IPv6 together.** A device or network uses IPv6 when both sides support it, and falls back to IPv4 otherwise. - **NAT/CGNAT lets many users share IPv4 addresses.** This is the “keep IPv4 functioning while transitioning” mechanism. - **Translation systems bridge IPv6-only and IPv4-only services.** For example, NAT64 can let an IPv6-only customer reach an older IPv4-only website. - **IPv4 transfer markets cover legacy needs.** Organizations that truly need public IPv4 can buy, lease, or transfer it from organizations with unused ranges. So the plan is not “create more IPv4 addresses.” That is impossible without breaking the protocol. It is: 1. preserve IPv4 for compatibility, 2. increasingly share scarce IPv4 addresses where necessary, 3. deploy IPv6 for new growth, 4. use translation during the long overlap. The weak point is that this plan is economically and operationally messy. IPv4 is deeply embedded in old systems, and NAT makes it possible to postpone IPv6 deployment. That is why the transition has taken decades. If IPv6 deployment stalled permanently, the Internet would still not suddenly run out and stop. Instead, new users and services would increasingly be placed behind shared IPv4 addresses, public IPv4 would become more expensive, inbound hosting would become harder, and more applications would need relays or workarounds. The result would be a progressively less clean and less capable Internet—not an instant failure. In that sense, you are right that there is no elegant global “endgame” for IPv4 alone. IPv4 itself has no solution to address scarcity. IPv6 is the solution; NAT, CGNAT, address markets, and translation are the transition and damage-control mechanisms.
