21:40:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: If by “actual plan” you mean a single authority with a binding, coordinated timetable saying “on date X, everyone switches from IPv4 to IPv6,” then you are right: **there is no such plan.** The Internet has no central operator that can order every ISP, company, government, device maker, and website to upgrade. IPv4 exhaustion was handled through decentralized policies and engineering choices, not a universal migration program. But that is different from saying there is *no plan at all*. The operational plan has been, and remains: - Regional registries stop issuing normal large IPv4 allocations once their free pools are depleted. - Networks use NAT and carrier-grade NAT to make limited public IPv4 space serve more customers. - Organizations transfer, buy, lease, and reclaim existing IPv4 blocks. - New network growth is supposed to happen primarily on IPv6. - Dual-stack and translation mechanisms keep IPv4-only and IPv6-capable systems interoperating. Crucially, this is not hypothetical: normal free IPv4 allocation has already been exhausted at the global and regional levels. The “running out” event happened gradually, not as a dramatic cliff. The Internet’s response was exactly the imperfect mixture above. So the honest version is: - **No:** there is no centrally enforced, complete, universally adopted transition plan. - **Yes:** there is an established technical and policy strategy for dealing with exhaustion—IPv6 deployment plus temporary/legacy IPv4 conservation mechanisms. - **Also yes:** it has been messy, slow, and dependent on individual operators choosing to deploy IPv6. The real risk is not that all existing IPv4 suddenly stops working. It is that continued reluctance to deploy IPv6 forces more users behind shared IPv4, raises costs, and makes some Internet functions—especially receiving unsolicited inbound connections—harder or dependent on relays.
