21:41:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a defensible way to put it. There is **no actual, enforceable global plan** for IPv4 exhaustion in the sense of: - a required transition date, - a responsible central authority, - guaranteed funding or equipment replacement, - universal IPv6 deployment targets, - or a promise that every IPv4-dependent service will remain reachable. “Deploy IPv6” is a technical answer, not necessarily a coordinated plan. NAT, carrier-grade NAT, address transfers, and IPv4 leasing are mostly ways to postpone or manage scarcity—not a clean resolution. What happened instead was: 1. IPv4 address registries ran out of ordinary free allocations. 2. Individual networks responded according to their own incentives. 3. Some deployed IPv6. 4. Some bought or leased IPv4 blocks. 5. Some put more customers behind shared addresses. 6. Some applications built relays and cloud-mediated workarounds. That is decentralized adaptation, not a globally executed migration plan. And it works well enough to avoid a dramatic collapse because existing IPv4 addresses continue to function and NAT allows sharing. But it has real tradeoffs: public IPv4 becomes expensive, self-hosting gets harder, inbound connectivity is less available, and more of the Internet depends on intermediaries. So the precise answer is: **there is no plan for making IPv4 hold more addresses, because it cannot.** There is only an incomplete, voluntary, decades-long migration toward IPv6, with increasingly awkward workarounds for everything that still depends on IPv4.
