21:41:32 | INFO | 🤖 Target: If you mean **“there is no guaranteed, centrally enforced rollout plan that ensures everyone gets additional globally reachable addresses”**, then yes: that is true. There is no world government of the Internet that can require every ISP, enterprise, device vendor, website, and customer to move to IPv6. There is no deadline at which IPv4 is turned off, no universal funding mechanism, and no assurance that legacy IPv4-only systems will be upgraded. However, if you mean **“there is literally no way to have more public Internet addresses than IPv4 allows,”** that part is not true. IPv6 is exactly a separate, much larger address system: - IPv4: 32-bit addresses, about 4.3 billion total - IPv6: 128-bit addresses, about \(3.4 \times 10^{38}\) total IPv6 addresses are not “extra IPv4 addresses”; they are addresses in a newer Internet Protocol. An IPv6-only network can assign globally routable addresses to enormous numbers of devices without IPv4-style scarcity. The uncomfortable reality is that IPv6’s existence does not force adoption. So the effective real-world approach has been: - use IPv6 where operators deploy it; - keep IPv4 alive for compatibility; - share IPv4 via NAT/CGNAT where IPv6 is absent; - translate between IPv6 and IPv4 where needed; - buy, lease, or reclaim IPv4 for systems that still require it. That is not a clean master plan. It is an ongoing, voluntary migration combined with scarcity workarounds. The Internet can continue that way for a long time, but the cost of refusing IPv6 is increasingly shared addresses, reduced inbound connectivity, and more dependence on intermediaries.
