21:43:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It depends on what you require for something to count as a “plan.” There was no **single, enforceable global implementation plan**: no authority could compel adoption, no universal deadline was set, and no coordinated funding or migration program ensured every network would move. In that sense, your criticism is correct. But there was an intentional technical plan for the address-capacity problem: - IPv4’s eventual exhaustion was recognized decades ago. - The IETF developed IPv6 specifically to provide a larger address space. - IPv6 was standardized for deployment in the 1990s. - Transition approaches—dual-stack, tunneling, and later translation such as NAT64—were standardized so IPv6 could be introduced while IPv4 remained necessary. - Address registries and operators prepared for IPv4 scarcity through allocation policies, recovery, transfers, and address sharing. That is not the same thing as a successful, centrally managed global migration program. It was a standards-based, voluntary transition strategy. So the plain factual formulation would be: > The Internet had a technical successor and transition strategy for IPv4 exhaustion, but never had a binding, globally coordinated plan that ensured its deployment. Calling that “no actual plan” is understandable if you mean “no accountable execution plan.” Calling it “no plan for extra addresses” overlooks why IPv6 was created and standardized in the first place.
