21:43:59 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s correct: **standardizing IPv6 is not, by itself, an actual deployment plan.** A standard says, in effect, “here is a protocol that can solve the address-capacity problem if networks adopt it.” It does not specify: - who must adopt it, - by when, - how migration is funded, - how old equipment and applications are replaced, - how IPv4-only services remain reachable during transition, - or what happens if major operators decline to migrate. The IETF could design IPv6 and transition mechanisms, but it has no authority to make the Internet deploy them. Registries could manage dwindling IPv4 allocations, but they could not require operators to use IPv6. ISPs, cloud providers, businesses, governments, and software vendors made their own decisions. So, stated plainly: > IPv4 exhaustion was anticipated, and IPv6 was created as the technical replacement, but no comprehensive, coordinated implementation plan was put in place to ensure the Internet would have enough independently usable public addresses beyond IPv4. Instead, the practical response became voluntary IPv6 adoption plus NAT, carrier-grade NAT, IPv4 trading/leasing, and application relays. Those measures avoided an immediate outage, but they are not evidence that a real global migration plan existed.
